[zulrah_flinch]
// UNWIRED ON PURPOSE (2026-09-30). The owner reports the snake blinking out for a tick and back
// when it is attacked or attacking, which started with the round that added this. Nothing about
// the import is wrong that can be seen from here - the frame group and its base are packed the
// same way as Zulrah other five - so this is a bisect, not a diagnosis: defend_anim is off the
// three records and the sequence is kept here so it costs one line to put back.
// OSRS seq 5808 - the last of Zulrah's ten sequences, and the one no previous round had a name for.
//
// WHAT IT IS. Nine frames of its own five-frame group (5697), thirty cycles - one tick exactly - no
// priority, no loop, and the frame list is a palindrome: 4,1,2,5,3,5,2,1,4. Rendered on the snake's
// own mesh it is the head staying exactly where it is while the jaw gapes wide and closes again.
// Nothing travels, nothing winds up. That is a BLOCK: short, symmetric, in place, and the one thing
// every attackable npc needs that an OSRS npc record cannot carry, because OSRS has no block-anim
// field at all - it is script-driven there, which is exactly why this seq was left over.
//
// IT CANNOT INTERRUPT AN ATTACK, which is what makes wiring it safe. The client only takes a new
// animation when its priority is at least the one already playing (Client.getNpcPosExtended, the
// 0x2 branch), and this has none while the spit is 6, the dive 8 and the rise 9. So it plays when
// the snake is between attacks and is thrown away when it is not.
frame1=anim_osrs_5697_4
delay1=3
frame2=anim_osrs_5697_1
delay2=3
frame3=anim_osrs_5697_2
delay3=3
frame4=anim_osrs_5697_5
delay4=3
frame5=anim_osrs_5697_3
delay5=6
frame6=anim_osrs_5697_5
delay6=3
frame7=anim_osrs_5697_2
delay7=3
frame8=anim_osrs_5697_1
delay8=3
frame9=anim_osrs_5697_4
delay9=3
